#{[17592186065345 #edb/ref 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the **square of a difference**:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x  + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2-10x+25$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{5}},$ and we get\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n  $$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065345 #edb/ref 1065 1855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065345 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "70396264-e3c3-50d9-94a4-95e8017dbfde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065345 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065360 #edb/ref 1064 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ For example, \n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1.$ First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}}  = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065360 #edb/ref 1065 3087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "73efd6db-4f12-5b29-bb69-8db83970c328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065360 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065440 #edb/ref 1064 "When we want to multiply two binomials, we take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x + 2),$ we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x + 2),$ and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x+2)$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}{x}}(x + 2) + {\\color{red}{3}}(x + 2) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 2x + 3x + 6 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2 + 5x + 6\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065440 #edb/ref 1065 8877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065440 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0cea50d1-e4ce-511f-821f-0e5e04b6eca1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065440 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186533988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Positive Integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073820 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d7d26bc9-6f89-5da4-b8a9-6be6f4280f81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073821 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592